#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115226 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7ad52ce-d408-5390-b91f-c855274f39ea" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Matrix Product Using the Distributive Law"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115227 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8feeacff-b07d-5f98-a765-4d8571674a7a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Matrix Product Using the Associative Law"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115228 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a870c4a-daa5-5df2-8968-d8bf9ac0bd75" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Matrix Multiplication"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116486 :tutorial/content "We have already seen some properties of matrix multiplication. For example, multiplying any $n \\times n$ square matrix $A$ by the identity matrix $I_n$ will leave it unchanged:\n\n$$\nA I_n = I_n A = A.\n$$\n\nAlso, matrix multiplication follows the **distributive laws**:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)C & =AC+BC, \\\\ C(A+B) & =CA+CB.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "60b5c83f-3d84-5140-80c7-c4640bea28b0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2997 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116487 :tutorial/content "If $A,$ $B,$ and $C$ are three matrices that are conformable for multiplication, then they follow the **associative law**:\n\n$$\n\\left( AB\\right) C = A\\left( BC\\right)\n$$\n\nSo, when we wish to compute a product $ABC,$ we don't always have to start by multiplying $AB.$ Alternatively, we can start by multiplying $BC,$ and then multiply $A$ by the result. \n\nSometimes rearranging the parentheses might make the computations slightly easier." :tutorial/id #uuid "31c195bb-9066-55bf-ae02-9de81da2b7bb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2998 :tutorial/title "The Associative Law for Matrix Multiplication"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116492 :tutorial/content "In general, matrix multiplication is **not** commutative. In other words, given two general matrices $A$ and $B$ that are conformable for multiplication, we have\n\n$$\nAB \\neq BA.\n$$\n\nHowever, some particular matrices may satisfy this property. For instance, $AI = IA$ (where $I$ is the identity matrix)." :tutorial/id #uuid "4e92439e-2fc7-59ca-beb8-2ace6a7d3e71" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3129 :tutorial/title "Matrix Multiplication Is Not Commutative in General"}]}
